# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env (real AI key, SMS provider login, DB password, JWT signing key) and
# create_admin_*.py (an admin phone + plaintext password) ARE committed
# here. That is only safe because this repository is private — if it is
# ever made public or shared, every value in them must be rotated first.
# .env.example documents the same variables without values.

# ── Local environment ────────────────────────────────────────────────
venv*/
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# ── Runtime data ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Teacher-uploaded avatars/sources and the rembg model download; mounted
# as a volume in production, never part of the image.
uploads/
logs/
backups/
*.db
*.sqlite3

# Local dev-server scratch (see the 8003 workaround in the app's
# api_constants.dart comment).
*.pid
run8003*.log
run_out.log
run_err.log
uvicorn_std*.log
sample_page.html
progress_ascii.txt

# ── Telegram payment bot secret ──────────────────────────────────────
# UNLIKE .env above, this file is NOT committed and must never be. The
# bot token is a bearer credential: anyone holding it can read every
# message sent to the bot, impersonate it, and reply as it. .env is
# committed here because the repo is private, but a token that has
# already been exposed once should not be stored anywhere a `git push`
# to the wrong remote would publish it.
#
# Put TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and the other TELEGRAM_* values in:
#     backend/.env.telegram
# app/telegram/config.py loads it after .env, so it wins.
.env.telegram
.env.telegram.*
*.env.secret
# ...but the template carries no values and must be committed, or nobody
# knows which variables to set.
!.env.telegram.example
